    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "Accurate and scalable aggregation of plastic-degrading enzyme activity data from academic literature is limited by the bottleneck of manual human curation found in resources like PAZY[cite: 1]. To overcome this constraint, I developed an automated, high-precision extraction pipeline powered by large language models to parse thousands of academic PDFs[cite: 1]. The system extracts activity measurements specifically for experimentally validated plastic-degrading enzymes while filtering out unverified targets[cite: 1].\n",
    "\n",
    "The pipeline utilizes an iterative multi-pass extraction architecture and targeted LLM verification passes to eliminate hallucinations, enforce entity grounding and recover missing values[cite: 1]. Synthetic controls and cross-model benchmarks were developed to quantify extraction precision, recall and relational integrity prior to full-scale deployment across literature corpora from the previous phase scraping[cite: 1]."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cell-objectives",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Objectives\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Engineer a multi-stage LLM extraction and verification pipeline using Claude Sonnet on AWS Bedrock to extract enzyme and experimental assay parameters with verbatim evidence grounding[cite: 2].\n",
    "2. Generate spiked synthetic controls within real paper to assess true positive capture rates, decoy rejection rates and relational pool linkage[cite: 2].\n",
    "3. Benchmark LLM models that I have access to (Sonnet, Haiku, Opus and hybrid configurations of the three) against a cross-model consensus truth pool to determine optimal accuracy and cost[cite: 2].\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Materials and Methods\n",
    "\n",
    "### System Initialization\n",
    "\n",
    "The pipeline was orchestrated on AWS Bedrock utilizing Claude 4.6 Sonnet, Haiku and Opus models[cite: 2]. Local and cloud compute environments integrated Python execution environments using regex modules, pandas and deterministic verification scripts to execute non-LLM validation and tabular cleaning steps[cite: 2].\n",
    "\n",
    "### Data Initialization\n",
    "\n",
    "```bash\n",
    "# Accessed: 2026-10-10\n",
    "# <command used to generate or download input data>\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Processing Steps\n",
    "\n",
    "**Phase 1: Multi-Stage Extraction and Verification Pipeline Development**[cite: 2]\n",
    "- Pass 1: Sonnet 4.6 generates a dossier of all the plastic degrading enzymes in the paper and the experiments those enzymes participated with the corresponding substrates in using chronological thinking[cite: 2, 3].\n",
    "- Pass 2: Using the dossier from pass 1 Sonnet 4.6 extracts the relevant protein and activity data along with the exact verbatim evidence sentence the value was pulled from[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Verification step 1: Using regex to check if the verbatim evidence sentences truly originated from the papers or were hallucinated[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Verification step 2: Using simple python, the enzyme-substrate combinations from the dossier in pass 1 is compared to the extracted list in pass 2, any new proteins are flagged for review and any missing proteins are added in a notes section for LLM review[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Verification step 3: A list of substrates is generated and compared against an exhaustive list of substrates to lightly flag any substrates not found in the list[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Verification step 4: Sonnet 4.6 checks to see if proteins are actually tested on plastic substrates and removes any proteins that aren't with associated protein data[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Rescue step 4.1: Sonnet 4.6 checks if any proteins that are tested against plastic/rubber substrates are not mentioned in the list and adds them if they are[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Verification step 5: Sonnet 4.6 checks if the values are correct and originate from the evidence sentence, replacing any incorrect values with either the correct value or \"Not_Found\"[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Rescue step 5.1: Sonnet 4.6 looks for their actual values and an accompanying evidence sentence for \"Not_Found\" values and verification step 4 is rerun[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Verification step 6: Sonnet 4.6 ensures that each \"relational pool\" is correctly associated, flagging any values that aren't[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Rescue step 6.1: Sonnet 4.6 takes the flagged values and associates them with the correct relational pool[cite: 3].\n",
    "\n",
    "**Phase 2: Synthetic Control Creation and Pipeline Fidelity Validation**[cite: 3]\n",
    "- Step 1: Inject synthetic protein entities, plausible activity measurements and non-degrading decoy proteins into 200 distinct academic papers[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Step 2: Process the spiked corpus through pipeline iterations (v1 baseline through final v16 multi-step rescue pipeline)[cite: 3].\n",
    "- Step 3: Quantify true positive capture rates, decoy false-positive acceptance rates and relational integrity linking assays to specific enzymes[cite: 3].\n",
    "\n",
    "**Phase 3: Model Benchmarking**[cite: 4]\n",
    "- Step 1: Evaluate extraction performance across Sonnet, Haiku, Opus and hybrid passes against a core truth pool (N=821 proteins)[cite: 4].\n",
    "- Step 2: Compare verbatim evidence sentence overlap and semantic concordance across models[cite: 4].\n",
    "\n",
    "**Phase 4: Production Corpus Execution**[cite: 4]\n",
    "- Deploy the finalized pipeline across ~4,000 relevant papers and execute post-extraction data cleaning (collapsing duplicate entries, filtering non-numeric text and flagging low-confidence tiers)[cite: 4].\n",
    "\n",
