    "## Results & Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "The finalized v16 extraction architecture operates across 8 LLM steps and 3 non-LLM steps at an approximate unit cost of $0.33 per paper[cite: 4]. Across synthetic spike-in evaluations, the pipeline achieved zero decoy contamination while maintaining near-complete capture of validated entities[cite: 4]. Model benchmarking demonstrated that Sonnet provided the optimal balance of recall, precision and global metric accuracy, whereas Opus suffered from severe under-extraction (9.1% recall)[cite: 4]. Production execution across 4,049 papers yielded 51,455 high-quality measurements across diverse polymer families[cite: 4].\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key metric**: 40,803 / 51,455 retained measurements (79%) _achieved high-confidence classification (retaining numeric value, verified unit and identified enzyme/substrate pair)_[cite: 4].\n",
    "\n",
    "**Breakdown by category**:\n",
    "**Synthetic Controls**\n",
    "- 100% decoy rejection rate (no false positives)[cite: 4]\n",
    "- 99.3% (152/153) Valid Spike-in Capture Rate (true positives)[cite: 4]\n",
    "- 100% (152/152) Relational linkage Assays and Proteins[cite: 4]\n",
    "- 98.8% (712/720) Assay Variable Extraction Rate[cite: 4]\n",
    "- 98.0% (196/200) Activity Metric Extraction Rate[cite: 5]\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cross-Model Benchmark Performance (N=821 consensus proteins)**\n",
    "- Sonnet: 95.0% Precision, 87.1% Recall, 90.9% F1 Score and 97.7% Global Metric Accuracy[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Haiku: 90.1% Precision, 81.5% Recall, 85.6% F1 Score and 94.4% Global Metric Accuracy[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Opus: 100.0% Precision, 9.1% Recall, 16.7% F1 Score and 98.1% Global Metric Accuracy[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Opus -> Sonnet Hybrid: 92.9% Precision, 80.0% Recall, 86.0% F1 Score and 98.5% Global Metric Accuracy[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Opus -> Haiku Hybrid: 96.2% Precision, 76.7% Recall, 85.4% F1 Score and 95.4% Global Metric Accuracy[cite: 5]\n",
    "\n",
    "**Production Corpus Overview (4049 papers)**\n",
    "- Cleaned Entities: 27,244 experiments, 31,441 enzymes (after deduplicating 419 records) and 51,455 cleaned measurement rows (from 61,771 raw rows)[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Primary Removal Drivers: 4,508 duplicate rows collapsed, 2,993 lacking measurement values, 2,354 descriptive texts, 325 missing enzymes, 111 implausible units and 25 implausible temperatures[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Coverage Distribution: Median 5 experiments per paper (max 80), median 10 retained measurements per paper and median 3 enzymes per paper[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Polymer Representation: PET & related polyesters (16,509 measurements; 32%), aliphatic polyesters (PCL/PLA/PHA/PBS; 15,026 measurements; 29%), polyolefins (3,429 measurements; 7%), polyurethane (2,698 measurements; 5%) and polystyrene (2,139 measurements; 4%)[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Substrate Physical Form: Film (11,438), powder (3,553), solution (1,500), emulsion (963), granules (549) and nanoparticles (536)[cite: 5]\n",
    "\n",
    "**Observations**:\n",
    "- Pairwise model comparisons revealed 100% verbatim evidence sentence correlation[cite: 5].\n",
    "- When different models isolated the same protein entity, their evidence spans and extracted values showed zero semantic contradiction[cite: 5].\n",
    "- Strict extraction prompting prioritized precision over recall, deliberately omitting ambiguous values to prevent synthetic hallucination[cite: 5].\n",
    "- Field completeness varied across experimental parameters: enzyme (100%) and substrate (99%) identification were near-universal, while assay environment parameters such as pH (40%), buffer composition (39%) and expression plasmid (8%) were less frequently reported or captured[cite: 5].\n",
    "- Models rarely hallucinate past the verification steps[cite: 5].\n",
    "\n",
    "**Follow-up questions**:\n",
    "- How will ingesting unstructured supplementary tables and files impact recall rates across buffer and plasmid fields?[cite: 5]\n",
    "- Will increasing input figure resolutions above 512x512 improve extraction of kinetic metrics presented strictly in graphical formats?[cite: 6]\n",
    "- What downstream gains in precision can be achieved by transferring unit standardization and mathematical conversions entirely to native Python modules?[cite: 6]\n",
    "- How well do the models actually compare against human data and how can they be improved?[cite: 6]"
